.TH AVOE 1 "September 2026" "avoe 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
avoe \- a small Emacs-like editor written in Ada
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B avoe
[\fB\-q\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-splash\fR] [\fIfile\fR ...]
.br
.B avoe
\fB\-\-batch\fR \fIscript.avoe\fR [\fIfile\fR ...]
.br
.B avoe
\fB\-\-paths\fR | \fB\-\-version\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B avoe
(Ada Version Of Emacs) is a text editor for the Linux terminal, written in
Ada 2012.  It follows the spirit of JOVE: the Emacs keys and ideas that
people use every day, in a small and fast program, and nothing more.  It
does not use
.IR ncurses ;
it puts the terminal in raw mode itself, writes ANSI escape sequences, and
redraws only the screen rows that changed.
.PP
A
.I buffer
holds the text of a file.  Several buffers can be open at once, and the
screen can be split into
.I windows
that each show a buffer.  The line at the bottom of every window is its
.IR "mode line" ,
which shows whether the buffer was changed, its name, where you are in it
(line and column, counting columns from 0) and its major mode.  The last
line of the screen is the
.IR "echo area" ,
where messages appear and where commands ask for file names, search
patterns and answers.
.PP
Every command has a name, such as
.BR find_file .
Keys are bound to those names, and
.B M-x
runs a command by name with TAB completion.  Scripts can define new
commands and bind them, so a script command and a built-in command are
used in the same way.
.PP
In this page
.B C-x
means Control-x, and
.B M-x
means Meta-x, which on most keyboards is Alt-x, or ESC followed by x.
.SH INVOKING AVOE
Named files are opened at startup; the first one is shown.  A file that
does not exist gives an empty buffer that is created when you save it.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-no\-init
Do not load the user's scripts and init file.  Bundled scripts and the
site configuration are still loaded.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-splash
Do not show the startup screen.
.TP
.BI \-\-batch " script"
Run an Avoe script without a terminal, then exit.  Messages go to standard
output and errors to standard error.  This is how scripts are tested and
how avoe can be used to change files from a shell script.
.TP
.B \-\-paths
Show where avoe looks for scripts and configuration, and which of those
files exist, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-\-help
Show the version, or a summary of these options, and exit.
.PP
At startup the screen is split: the upper window shows
.BR *About* ,
a read-only buffer with the version, the author and a few keys, and the
cursor is in the lower window with your file.
.B C-x 1
closes it,
.B \-\-no\-splash
skips it for one run, and
.B "Set_Startup_Screen (False);"
in the init file turns it off for good.
.SH GETTING STARTED
.B C-x C-f
opens a file,
.B C-x C-s
saves it and
.B C-x C-c
leaves the editor.
.B C-g
cancels whatever is going on: a prompt, a search, or a command that asks
for more keys.
.PP
Most commands take a repeat count.
.B C-u
followed by digits and a command runs it that many times, so
.B "C-u 40 -"
inserts forty dashes.
.B C-u
without digits means four.
.PP
While a command asks for something in the echo area, the usual editing
keys work there too: C-a, C-e, C-b, C-f and the arrow keys move, DEL and
C-d delete, M-DEL deletes a word, C-k kills to the end, C-u clears the
line, C-y yanks, TAB completes file names, buffer names and command names,
and M-p and M-n (or Up and Down) walk through what you typed there
earlier.  RET accepts and C-g gives up.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
.B "M-x describe_bindings"
lists every binding,
.B "M-x describe_key"
says what one key does, and
.B "M-x apropos_command"
lists the commands whose names contain a word.
.SS Moving around
.TP 16
.B C-f, C-b
one character forward or back
.TP
.B C-n, C-p
one line down or up, keeping the column
.TP
.B M-f, M-b
one word forward or back
.TP
.B C-a, C-e
start or end of the line
.TP
.B M-m
back to the first non-blank character of the line
.TP
.B M-<, M->
start or end of the buffer
.TP
.B C-v, M-v
one screen forward or back
.TP
.B M-{, M-}
start or end of the paragraph
.TP
.B C-M-f, C-M-b
over a bracketed group; the matching bracket is underlined
.TP
.B C-l
put the current line in the middle of the window
.TP
.B M-g g
go to a line by number
.PP
The arrow keys, Home, End, PgUp and PgDn do what you would expect, and
Ctrl with the left and right arrows moves by words.
.SS Changing text
.TP 16
.B RET, C-o
break the line, or open a line below
.TP
.B C-j
break the line and indent the new one
.TP
.B DEL, C-d
delete the character before or under the cursor
.TP
.B M-DEL, M-d
kill the word before or after the cursor
.TP
.B C-k
kill to the end of the line; again to kill the line break
.TP
.B M-z
kill up to and including a character you type
.TP
.B C-t, M-t
swap the characters, or the words, around the cursor
.TP
.B C-x C-t
swap this line with the one above
.TP
.B M-\e, M-SPC
remove the blanks around the cursor, or leave exactly one
.TP
.B M-^
join this line to the one above
.TP
.B M-u, M-l, M-c
upper case, lower case or capitalise the word
.TP
.B C-x C-u, C-x C-l
upper or lower case the region
.TP
.B M-q
rewrap the paragraph or comment to the fill column
.TP
.B M-;
comment or uncomment the line, or the region
.TP
.B M-/
complete the word before the cursor from the words in your buffers;
repeat it for the next suggestion
.TP
.B C-q
insert the next key as a character, even a control character
.TP
.B "C-_, C-x u"
undo; repeat to go further back.  Any other command in between lets you
undo the undo
.SS The mark, killing and yanking
.TP 16
.B C-SPC
set the mark here.  The text between the mark and the cursor is the
.IR region .
.TP
.B C-x C-x
swap the cursor and the mark
.TP
.B C-x h
the whole buffer becomes the region
.TP
.B C-w, M-w
kill or copy the region
.TP
.B C-y
yank back the last killed text
.TP
.B M-y
right after C-y, replace it with an older kill
.TP
.B M-=
count the lines, words and characters in the region, or in the buffer
.PP
Killing several times in a row collects the text into one entry, so
.B "C-k C-k C-k"
followed by
.B C-y
moves three lines.
.SS Searching and replacing
.TP 16
.B C-s, C-r
incremental search forward or back.  Type to extend it, C-s or C-r again
for the next match, DEL to take back the last character, RET to stop where
you are, C-g to go back to where you started.  A search without upper-case
letters ignores case.
.TP
.B C-M-s, C-M-r
the same with a regular expression
.TP
.B M-%
query replace.  At each match: y or SPC replaces, n or DEL skips, ! does
all the rest, . replaces and stops, q or RET stops.
.PP
.B "M-x replace_string" ,
.B "M-x replace_regexp"
and
.B "M-x query_replace_regexp"
work from the cursor to the end of the buffer.  In a regexp replacement
\fB\e1\fR to \fB\e9\fR stand for the bracketed groups and \fB\e&\fR for the
whole match.  Regular expressions are Perl-like and match within one line.
.SS Files, buffers and windows
.TP 16
.B C-x C-f
find a file
.TP
.B C-x C-s, C-x C-w
save, or save under another name
.TP
.B C-x s
offer to save every changed buffer
.TP
.B C-x i
insert another file here
.TP
.B C-x C-q
make the buffer read-only, or writable again
.TP
.B C-x b, C-x C-b
switch to a buffer, or list the buffers
.TP
.B C-x k
kill a buffer
.TP
.B C-x 2, C-x 1, C-x 0
split the window, keep only this one, or close this one
.TP
.B C-x o
go to the other window
.TP
.B C-x C-c
leave, offering to save changed files
.PP
.B "M-x revert_buffer"
reads the file again and throws away your changes, and
.B "M-x recover_file"
brings back what was auto-saved.
.SS Keyboard macros
.TP 16
.B C-x (
start recording the keys you press
.TP
.B C-x )
stop recording
.TP
.B C-x e
run the macro; press e again to run it once more.  With a repeat count,
.B "C-u 20 C-x e"
runs it twenty times.
.PP
What you type in the echo area is recorded too, so a macro can search,
answer questions and run M-x commands.
.SS Compiling and shell commands
.TP 16
.B C-c C-c
compile (in Ada, Avoe script, C, Python and shell buffers)
.TP
.B "C-x `, M-g n, M-g p"
go to the next or previous error
.TP
.B C-c C-o
switch between a body and its spec, or a .c and its .h
.TP
.B C-c C-f
run the mode's source formatter on the buffer
.TP
.B M-!
run a shell command and show its output; with C-u, insert the output
.TP
.B M-|
send the region to a shell command; with C-u, replace it with the output
.PP
The build runs in the background, and you can keep editing while its
output arrives in the
.B *compilation*
buffer.  There, RET visits the error on the line, g compiles again and
C-c C-k kills the build.
.B "M-x grep"
puts search results in the same buffer, so the same keys walk through
them.
.SS Help and the rest
.TP 16
.B M-x
run a command by name
.TP
.B C-u
repeat count for the next command
.TP
.B C-g
cancel
.TP
.B C-x =
describe the character under the cursor and where it is
.TP
.B M-:
evaluate an Avoe script expression or statement
.TP
.B C-z
suspend the editor; fg in the shell brings it back
.PP
.B "M-x about_avoe"
shows the version and the keys from the startup screen again.
.SH MAJOR MODES
Each buffer has a major mode, chosen from the file name and shown in the
mode line: Ada
.RI ( .adb ", " .ads ", " .ada ", " .gpr ),
Avoe script
.RI ( .avoe ", " .avoerc ),
C
.RI ( .c ", " .h ),
Python
.RI ( .py ", " .pyw ),
shell
.RI ( .sh ", " .bash ", " .bashrc " and others),"
Compilation for build output, and Fundamental for everything else.
.B "M-x set_major_mode"
changes it by hand.
.PP
A mode decides the syntax highlighting, what TAB and RET do, which
formatter
.B C-c C-f
runs, and what
.B M-;
uses as a comment.  In Ada and Avoe script, TAB re-indents the line and
RET re-indents it and indents the next one.  The C, Python and shell modes
are written in Avoe script and live in
.IR PREFIX/share/avoe/ ,
so they can be read as examples and changed.
.SH EXTENDING AVOE
Avoe script is a small Ada-flavoured language: blocks and names look like
Ada, but declarations are optional and values carry their type at run
time.  A procedure with
.B "pragma Command;"
becomes an editor command, and
.B Bind_Key
gives it keys:
.PP
.RS
.nf
procedure Duplicate_Line is
   pragma Command;
   Text : constant String := Current_Line;
begin
   End_Of_Line;
   Insert (LF & Text);
end Duplicate_Line;

Bind_Key ("C-c d", "duplicate_line");
.fi
.RE
.PP
Settings are ordinary calls in the init file, among them
.BR "Set_Backups (False)" ,
.BR "Set_Auto_Save (Keys => 100, Idle_Seconds => 10)" ,
.BR "Set_Confirm_Exit (True)" ,
.BR "Set_Line_Numbers (True)" ,
.BR "Set_Startup_Screen (False)" ,
.B "Set_Fill_Column (78)"
and
.BR "Set_Tab_Width (4)" .
.B "M-x load_file" ,
.B "M-x eval_buffer"
and
.B M-:
run script code in a running editor; C-g interrupts a script that takes
too long.  The language and every built-in function are described in
.IR PREFIX/share/doc/avoe/avoe\-script.md .
.SH SAVING, BACKUPS AND RECOVERY
A save writes a temporary file next to the original and renames it into
place, so an interrupted save never leaves a half-written file.
Permissions are kept, and saving through a symbolic link writes the file
it points to.  The first save of a file in a session keeps the previous
contents as
.IR file~ .
.PP
Unsaved changes are written to the auto-save directory every 300
keystrokes and after 30 seconds without typing.  If avoe or the machine
dies, opening the file again offers to bring those changes back;
.B "M-x recover_file"
does it at any time.  Auto-save files are removed when you save and when
you leave normally.
.PP
If another program changes a file you are visiting, avoe reloads it when
you have no unsaved changes, and warns you when you do; saving then asks
first.
.SH LIMITS
A buffer holds about 1 GiB.  Undo remembers the most recent 20,000
changes, or 16 MB of deleted text.  Lines longer than 4,096 bytes are
shown without syntax colours.  A regular expression matches within one
line.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
avoe exits with status 0 when all is well, and 1 when it cannot use the
terminal or when a
.B \-\-batch
script fails.  If a command fails because of a bug, the editor keeps
running, says
.BR "Internal error" ,
and appends the details and a traceback to
.IR ~/.local/state/avoe/internal\-errors.log .
Please send that with a bug report.
.SH FILES
Files are loaded at startup in this order; later ones can override earlier
ones.
.TP
.I PREFIX/share/avoe/*.avoe
Bundled scripts, including the language modes. Found relative to the
executable.
.TP
.IR PREFIX/etc/avoe/site.avoe " or " /etc/avoe/site.avoe
Site-wide configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.config/avoe/scripts/*.avoe
The user's own scripts and modes.
.TP
.IR ~/.config/avoe/init.avoe " or " ~/.avoerc
The user's init file.
.TP
.I file~
Backup of a file's previous contents, made on its first save in a session.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/avoe/auto-save/
Auto-saved unsaved changes, offered for recovery after a crash.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/avoe/internal-errors.log
Details and tracebacks of internal errors, for bug reports.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AVOE_DATA_DIR
Use this directory instead of \fIPREFIX/share/avoe\fR.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base of the user configuration directory (default \fI~/.config\fR).
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Base of the user state directory (default \fI~/.local/state\fR).
.TP
.B TERM
The terminal type.  avoe writes plain ANSI sequences and does not read
.IR terminfo .
